# Line-ending policy.
# Batch files MUST be CRLF or cmd.exe mis-parses them (a LF .bat makes `@echo off`
# fail and comment lines execute as commands). Everything else stays LF so the Python,
# JSON and Markdown files git tracks are byte-stable across machines and the working
# tree stops warning "LF will be replaced by CRLF" on every touch. No .tscn is tracked
# (converted levels stay in the gitignored out/); the rule stays for a hand-authored
# fixture in the level format the converter writes and the Supersonic port reads.

*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

*.tscn  text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf

# Dotfiles have no extension, so the rules above miss them and `text=auto` below
# normalises them to LF in the repo while checking them out as CRLF — which makes
# git warn "LF will be replaced by CRLF" on every single touch. Pin them.
.gitignore     text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.gitmodules    text eol=lf

# LICENSE has no extension either. Without this line it falls under `text=auto` below and a checkout with core.autocrlf=true (the default on Windows, and on GitHub's Windows runners)
# writes it with CRLF, while NOTICE.md beside it stays LF; release.py copies both into every archive unchanged. tools/ci/test_notice.py pins it.
LICENSE        text eol=lf

# The licence texts of the interface languages' faces are .txt files, so they fall under `text=auto` below too and a Windows checkout writes them with CRLF; the content package
# copies them unchanged, and a package must be byte-identical whichever host built it.
game/data/fonts/*.txt text eol=lf

# Let git decide for anything not listed.
*       text=auto
